  "aiDomainContext": "## Head Coach knowledge\nT20 gives each side 20 overs, so every ball is worth something and role value is about impact per ball.\n- Powerplay hitting: in the first 6 overs only two fielders may stand outside the circle, so top-order batters who attack there add outsized runs.\n- Death-over bowling: overs 17 to 20 are the hardest to bowl; bowlers who keep economy low and take wickets there are scarce and valuable.\n- Finisher role: a lower-order batter who scores quickly under pressure. Strike rate matters more than volume.\n- Keeper-batter scarcity: a wicket-keeper who bats in the top six is rare, and every squad must carry at least 2 keepers, so the good ones are bid up.\n- Venue and conditions: small grounds and flat pitches favour batters and punish bowlers; slow, turning pitches favour spinners and economy.\n- Overseas-slot opportunity cost: a squad may hold at most 8 overseas players, so a very expensive overseas player uses a scarce slot that a cheaper domestic player could fill.\n## Stat glossary\nRates are held to 2 dp. A substitute who bats, bowls or fields counts as in the playing XI (impact player).\n- in-playing-xi: 1 when the player was in the playing XI or came on as an impact-player substitute and batted, bowled or fielded; otherwise 0.\n- runs: runs the batter scored.\n- balls-faced: legal balls the batter faced.\n- fours: boundaries hit for four.\n- sixes: boundaries hit for six.\n- duck: 1 when a batter was dismissed without scoring, else 0.\n- strike-rate: runs per 100 balls faced, supplied by the data feed or entered by hand, to 2 dp.\n- wickets: dismissals credited to the bowler; each can carry a type such as bowled, lbw, caught, stumped or hit-wicket.\n- balls-bowled: legal balls the bowler delivered, as a whole number; 2 overs is 12 balls.\n- runs-conceded: runs scored off the bowler.\n- maidens: overs in which the bowler conceded no runs.\n- economy: runs conceded per over, supplied or entered by hand, to 2 dp.\n- catches: catches taken by a fielder.\n- stumpings: stumpings made by the keeper.\n- run-outs: run-outs a fielder took part in, typed direct (own throw or hit) or assisted.\n- player-of-match: 1 for the match's Player of the Match, else 0.\n## Fit heuristics\n- Slot scarcity: the squad needs at least 2 keepers, 5 batters, 5 bowlers and 3 all-rounders; weigh a bid against the slots still to fill, not only the player's quality.\n- Overseas-slot opportunity cost: with 8 overseas slots, compare an overseas bid with the best domestic option for the same role.\n- Keeper-batter scarcity: a keeper who bats high is worth a premium because few exist.\n- Balance: a squad heavy in one role leaves too few strong bowlers or batters for the Playing XI.\n- Purse pacing: spending too much early leaves too little for the minimum squad of 18."
